#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064150 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d01201-07ca-5083-a7f8-095be503df27" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Point Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e6c4219b-1955-5fa0-a362-6cc273510d88" :question/math-academy-id "e-258" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n${\\sqrt{bx + 3},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 2, \\\\ 5,\\end{vmatrix}x = 2$.\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$, what is the value $b$?" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x)$,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → 2)g(x) = lim_(x → 2)(\\sqrt{bx + 3}) = \\sqrt{2b + 3}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)g(x) &= g(2) \\\\\n\\sqrt{2b + 3} &= 5 \\\\\n2b + 3 &= 25 \\\\\n2b &= 22 \\\\\nb &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50eadb82-ffa5-5ee5-b878-1c04e41fba7e" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Point Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b411bf9f-f944-505d-94dc-13df743545dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-259" :question/problem "The function $h(x)$ is given by\n${\\log_{3} (x + c),\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 0, \\\\ 2,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0$.\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, what is the value of $c$?" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $h(x)$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)h(x) &= lim_(x → 0)\\log_{3} (x + c) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (0 + c) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (c)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)h(x) &= h(0) \\\\\n\\log_{3} (c) &= 2 \\\\\nc &= 3^{2} \\\\\nc &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $c=9$ removes the point discontinuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064118 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c931e4a8-ad1d-5a5a-8f8f-f3901efda408" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Point Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5443849c-193c-58a9-ae6c-1b859bbd1d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6320" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n${x^{2} + 2kx + 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1, \\\\ 4,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1$.\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)(x^{2} + 2kx + 1) \\\\\n&= 1^{2} + 2k(1) + 1 \\\\\n&= 2 + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\\\\n2 + 2k &= 4 \\\\\n2k &= 2 \\\\\nk &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=1$ removes the point discontinuity."}]}
